course: dictionary
lesson: 652
title: Two meanings of badger
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Badger is a noun, a thing or animal, and a verb, a doing word. Context tells you which meaning the writer wants. «A stripy head, with high shoulders behind it» peered out of a hedge. A head peering out of a hedge is an animal, so here badger means the wild creature.
explain: Look at the words around the word. In «Badger trusts Mole with one more job», Badger is a name, a character who acts like a friend. Capital letters can be a clue too, like Badger's name in a story.
explain: Now look at a verb: «Stop badgering me!» Try your meaning in the sentence. Does «Stop asking me again and again until I get annoyed!» make sense? It does, so badger here means to keep asking over and over.
fact: A badger is a wild animal with grey fur, short legs and a black and white striped face.
fact: Badgers live in tunnels underground and come out at night.
fact: A badger's home is called a sett, or burrow, and badgers eat worms, insects and roots.
fact: To badger someone means to keep asking them again and again for something until they get annoyed.
fact: Badger can also be a name in a story, as in «You're so eloquent, dear Badger».
exercise: «A stripy head, with high shoulders behind it» peered out of a hedge. What kind of creature is this? | A fox with a long tail | A hedgehog | *A badger | A mole digging a tunnel
exercise: Nadia says, «I'm going to badger Dad for a treat!» What does badger mean here? | To dig a sett underground | To creep quietly away | To sleep all day in a tunnel | *To keep asking again and again until someone gives in
exercise: «Stop badgering me, I'm trying to read!» What does this mean? | Dig a tunnel in the garden | Feed me some worms | *Stop asking me again and again | Come out and play at night
test: What is a badger's home called? | A nest | *A sett | A hive | A kennel
test: Which sentence uses badger as a verb? | «Badger trusts Mole with one more job» | *«Badgers keep badgering Mum for sweets» | «Badger's great secret: there is an underground passage» | «A stripy head peered out of a hedge»
exam: In «Badger trusts Mole with one more job», what does Badger mean? | A striped animal that digs setts | *A character's name, a friend in the story | To keep asking again and again | A badge pinned on a jacket
